---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CURRENCY_META, fxNote, type Currency } from '../../lib/currency';
import { dataFor, realReturnsFor } from '../../lib/data';
import { currencyPaths } from '../../lib/routes';

export const getStaticPaths = currencyPaths;

const { currency } = Astro.props as { currency: Currency };
const {
  riskMetrics,
  correlations,
  signals,
  btcHistory,
  btcDaily,
  benchmarksDaily,
  benchmarksHistory,
} = dataFor(currency);
const meta = CURRENCY_META[currency];
const realReturns = realReturnsFor(currency);

// Which ticker actually served ETH, read from the committed file rather than
// asserted. The GBP tree takes ETH natively where every other series there is
// converted, and a silent fallback to conversion would make the paragraph
// below false — the exact failure this page exists to avoid.
const ethBenchmark = benchmarksDaily.benchmarks.find((b) => b.asset === 'eth');
const ethNative = ethBenchmark?.sourceSeries.endsWith('-GBP') ?? false;
// The divergence figures are read from the file the run wrote, not written
// here. They were prose literals for one commit — including a 95th percentile
// the check did not compute — which is exactly what this page's opening
// paragraph promises never happens.
const div = benchmarksDaily.ethQuoteDivergence;

/**
 * Figures quoted below come from the committed data, not from prose. Every
 * number on this page that could drift is read from the file that produced it
 * — the alternative is a methodology page that describes a version of the
 * pipeline that no longer runs, which is worse than having none.
 */
const volWindows = riskMetrics.rollingVol.map((w) => w.windowDays).join('/');
const corrWindow = correlations.windowDays;
const t = signals.thresholds;
---

<Base
  currency={currency}
  title="blockplot · methodology"
  description="Every formula the site computes, the source each reads, and what each one deliberately does not claim."
>
  <h1 class="visually-hidden">Methodology</h1>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>How to read this</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Every figure on this site is computed by a pure function in <code>pipeline/</code>, unit-tested
      against fixed fixtures with exact expected outputs, and committed to <code>/data</code> as
      versioned JSON by a scheduled job. Pages render that file and do no arithmetic of their own.
      This page is the whole set of formulas in one place; each section also carries its own note.
      Figures quoted here are read from the committed data, so they cannot describe a pipeline that
      no longer runs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <dl class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>Daily close</dt>
        <dd class="num">CoinGecko</dd>
        <dd class="sub num">{btcDaily.series.length} days · to {btcDaily.stats.latestDate}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>Deep history</dt>
        <dd class="num">blockchain.com</dd>
        <dd class="sub num">{btcHistory.series.length} days · from {btcHistory.series[0]?.date}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>Benchmarks</dt>
        <dd class="num">Yahoo / FRED</dd>
        <dd class="sub num">S&amp;P 500, gold, DXY, ETH</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>Rolling vol reads</dt>
        <dd class="num">{riskMetrics.rollingVolSource}</dd>
        <dd class="sub num">full history, not the 365d window</dd>
      </div>
      {/* The deflator's identity is read from the dataset, never written here:
          the pipeline picks it from an ordered candidate list, so a literal
          would state the intended series rather than the one that answered. */}
      {realReturns && (
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>CPI deflator</dt>
          <dd class="num">{realReturns.deflator.sourceSeries}</dd>
          <dd class="sub num">
            FRED · to {realReturns.deflator.lastMonth} · {realReturns.deflator.lagMonths}m lag
          </dd>
        </div>
      )}
    </dl>
    <p class="method-note">
      Two BTC price sources, deliberately. CoinGecko's keyless tier caps history at 365 days, which
      is enough for the risk window but not for halving cycles or an all-time high, so a second
      series carries the full history. They disagree by a fraction of a percent on any given day —
      different exchange sets, different snapshot times — so each figure is labelled with the source
      it read rather than the two being quietly reconciled. Where both could serve, the deeper
      series wins.{fxNote(currency)}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Returns and volatility</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      <strong>Daily return</strong> is the log return <code>ln(Pt / Pt−1)</code>. Log rather than
      simple because they sum over time, which is what makes an annualisation meaningful.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      <strong>Realised volatility</strong> over a window is the sample standard deviation of those
      log returns, annualised by <code>√365</code> — 365 and not 252, because BTC trades every day.
      Benchmarks annualise by <code>√252</code> for the same reason in reverse. Windows: {volWindows} days.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      <strong>Sharpe</strong> is mean return over standard deviation, annualised, at a zero risk-free
      rate — stated rather than assumed, since a non-zero rate would change the ranking between
      assets. <strong>Sortino</strong> replaces the denominator with downside deviation, counting
      only negative returns. Both are null rather than zero when undefined: either with fewer than two
      returns, and then Sharpe additionally when the variance is zero, Sortino when there are no
      negative returns at all to form a downside deviation from.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Drawdown</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      <code>Pt / max(P0..Pt) − 1</code>, per day, against the <em>running</em> peak rather than a
      fixed one — so a drawdown is always measured from the highest close seen up to that day, and
      is zero on any day that sets a new high. The headline maximum keeps the first date it was
      reached, not the last.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Halving cycles</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Each cycle is indexed by days since its halving and normalised to a multiple of that day's
      close, which is what makes four cycles comparable on one axis. Cycles have different lengths
      and the current one is incomplete, so a chart position late in the x-axis legitimately shows
      fewer than four lines — an absent cycle is omitted rather than drawn at zero.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Correlation</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Pearson correlation of daily log returns over a rolling {corrWindow}-day window. Two
      corrections matter more than the formula:
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      BTC closes at 00:00 UTC while equities and metals close at 21:00 UTC the previous session, so
      BTC is re-dated to the session it actually shares before pairing. Without that, BTC's −49.7%
      on the snapshot dated 13 March 2020 met the S&amp;P's +9.29% rebound while its flat 12th met the
      S&amp;P's −9.51%: one inverted outlier pair then dominated a 60-observation window for three
      months, and the site reported BTC and equities as <em>inverse</em> through the most famous
      co-crash on record. Correcting the offset flips that window's sign — the magnitude depends on
      when you look, since it is a trailing figure, so the number is on the correlation page rather
      than quoted here; the sign flip is what a unit test pins.
      And the re-dating happens <em>before</em> currency conversion, not after: conversion is itself
      a per-date join, so re-dating afterwards leaves the two legs on different days and the FX term
      stops cancelling — measured at 0.16 of noise in GBP correlations, against a 0.25 threshold.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      Ether is dated differently from Bitcoin here, and the difference is measured rather than
      assumed. The BTC series is a snapshot at 00:00 UTC, so the point dated <em>d</em> is really
      the close of <em>d−1</em> and has to be shifted. Ether arrives from Yahoo, which dates a
      daily crypto bar by the day it covers — checked by comparing Yahoo's own BTC quote against
      the committed CoinGecko series across 365 overlapping days, where a one-day forward shift
      matched to a median 0.019% against 1.285% unshifted. So ETH is already on the session it
      closes and is left alone. Shifting it as well would put back exactly the offset the BTC
      shift removes, and it would surface as BTC and ETH looking less alike than they are.
    </p>
  </section>

  {ethBenchmark && (
  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Ether in {meta.label}</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      {
        currency === 'usd'
          ? 'Ether is a benchmark here rather than a second asset tree: it appears in the correlation matrix and the risk comparison, and no page is built around it. It annualises on 365 days like BTC rather than 252 like the market-hours assets, because it trades every day — using the market base would overstate its volatility by about a fifth while still looking like a plausible number.'
          : ethNative
            ? 'One deliberate inconsistency, stated plainly. Every other figure in the GBP tree is a re-denomination of a single USD source, converted at each day\'s rate. Ether is not: it is quoted natively as ETH-GBP, from its own market, because a sterling holder\'s ether really does trade in sterling. BTC is converted; ETH is not.'
            : 'Ether is normally quoted natively here as ETH-GBP, from its own market. That quote was unavailable when this data was built, so the figures on this page came from ETH-USD converted at each day\'s rate — the same route every other series in this tree takes.'
      }
    </p>
    {
      currency !== 'usd' && ethNative && (
        <p class="method-note">
          The cost of that choice is that two markets can disagree, so the pipeline measures it
          every run: the natively quoted series is compared against the converted one on every date
          both carry. {
            div
              ? `Over the ${div.days.toLocaleString('en-US')} shared days in this file the median gap is ${div.medianPct}%, the 95th percentile ${div.p95Pct}%, and the widest single day ${div.maxPct}% on ${div.maxDate}; ${div.beyond1Pct} of them sit beyond 1%.`
              : 'This file carries no such measurement — either it was built before the check existed, or one of the two routes was unavailable when it ran.'
          } The build fails if the <em>median</em> passes {div?.bandPct ?? 1}%, and only reports the
          worst day. That split is the point: a median that far out cannot be moved by one bad
          quote, so reaching it means something systematic is wrong — the wrong ticker, an inverted
          rate, a mis-joined date. A single wide day is two real markets on a bad afternoon, and
          failing on it would make this site hostage to a data provider's worst quote.
        </p>
        <p class="method-note">
          Two limits on that check, stated because they are easy to assume away. It compares every
          shared date including weekends, since both series trade through them — so a share of
          those days is converted at a rate carried over from Friday, and the median is a little
          easier than a weekday-only one would be. And a median is the wrong instrument for a stale
          rate feed: a stale tail is a minority of the record by construction and cannot move the
          middle of the distribution however wrong it is. That case is covered separately, by a
          check on how far the rate series lags the price series, which warns rather than failing.
        </p>
      )
    }
    <p class="method-note">
      The ticker that actually served this file is <code>{ethBenchmark.sourceSeries}</code>, read
      from the committed data rather than asserted here. This whole section is absent when no ETH
      source answered, rather than describing a series the file does not carry.
    </p>
  </section>
  )}

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Signals</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Band states are hysteretic. A band is entered only once {t.confirmDays} consecutive
      observations agree, and is then dated to the first of them — the day the change began, not the
      day it became certain. On this {meta.label} series a bare threshold test would give{' '}
      {signals.rawSpans.vol} volatility spans and {signals.rawSpans.drawdown} drawdown spans over the
      same window; confirmation is what turns that into a state worth reporting. A candidate that
      has not yet earned the switch is published as pending rather than hidden.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      Volatility bands: below {t.volLowPct}% and above {t.volHighPct}% annualised, on the{' '}
      {t.volWindowDays}-day window. Drawdown bands: {t.drawdownBandsPct.join('%, ')}% from the
      running peak, deepest reached. The same confirmation machine drives the correlation regimes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>DCA and holdings</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      The simulator buys at the close on each scheduled date, less a per-buy fee; a date missing
      from the data buys on the next available day, month-end schedules clamp, and two schedules
      landing together collapse into one buy. Undeployed cash counts toward wealth so both
      strategies start from the same budget and the comparison is like-for-like. Taxes, slippage and
      yield on idle cash are ignored.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      A holdings line holds the BTC amount <em>constant</em> across history. The site does not know
      when any of it was bought, so this is what that much BTC was worth — not a reconstruction of a
      position. A line implying a purchase history the data cannot support would be worse than no
      line. What you enter stays in your browser; see the note on the holdings page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Performance comparison</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Every series on the performance page is divided by its own close on a shared base date and
      multiplied by 100. The base is the first day on which every drawn series has a price, not the
      day the range button names — BTC and ether trade every day where the S&amp;P 500 and gold do
      not, so a weekend start would index the crypto legs before the market ones and bake that
      difference into every later point. A series whose history begins after the chosen start is
      excluded and named rather than drawn from partway across.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      That chart reads its own file, at mixed resolution: the last
      {benchmarksHistory.dailyDays} days daily, everything older one point per ISO week, keeping
      each week's last close rather than an average — an average is a value no market printed. The
      rule is chosen by measurement: a decade of five daily series is 92 KB gzipped, all of which
      must be embedded because the start date is chosen in the browser and this site makes no
      request to fetch more. Closes there are rounded to 6 significant figures, which moves the
      displayed index by less than one part in a million; the unrounded series stay in
      <code>btc-price-history.json</code> for the metrics that need them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Rounding, and what is deliberately not rounded</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Rounding is applied at the serialization edge — 2 dp for percentages and money, 8 dp for BTC,
      4 dp for cycle multiples — never mid-calculation. Two series are kept unrounded on purpose:
      converted closes, because a 2 dp round on a sub-pound 2010 price is an error of up to 11% that
      would propagate into the monthly heatmap; and the holdings value series, because rounding
      zeroes every early day for a small stack and a log axis then drops them off the chart.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
